1
00:00:00,060 --> 00:00:02,980
White attacks the queen, and Black attacks the king.

2
00:00:04,050 --> 00:00:07,190
This is the Traxler Counterattack in the Two Knights Defense.

3
00:00:08,040 --> 00:00:12,210
The first moves look normal. Develop, challenge the center, and wait.

4
00:00:12,520 --> 00:00:14,750
Natural moves hide the real idea.

5
00:00:16,610 --> 00:00:20,180
The pieces point at the target. One loose defender is enough.

6
00:00:20,700 --> 00:00:24,630
The bait appears. It looks free, but it is the trap's waiting move.

7
00:00:24,940 --> 00:00:28,010
The reply is forcing. Calculate the next check.

8
00:00:29,030 --> 00:00:34,500
The point is simple: The queen check tears open the king after both knights sacrifice.

9
00:00:34,810 --> 00:00:38,530
The king has fewer choices. Every escape square is covered.

10
00:00:38,900 --> 00:00:43,310
And now the queen check tears open the king after both knights sacrifice.

11
00:00:43,620 --> 00:00:47,340
The tempting move fails because the position was built for this tactic.

12
00:00:47,710 --> 00:00:50,900
Save the pattern. Spotting it one move earlier is the point.

13
00:00:51,800 --> 00:00:53,640
Would you see it before taking the bait?

